 **Many views in one page.** A page may open a view for each of its texts, a diff viewer's runs or a list of comments. The views of a page share what belongs to the page: one stylesheet, with the built-ins' rules once, and one highlighter, whose grammars load once. A view of text and code changes nothing in your page's styles when it opens, renders or goes, so what a view costs is its own content.
 
+**Your page's policy.** What the view renders in your page needs no inline styles: the boxes' rules are constructed stylesheets, highlighted code and math are styled through the CSSOM, so a page with `style-src 'self'`, without `'unsafe-inline'`, refuses nothing of CompMD's. Its `frame-src` must allow where the frames are served (`base`), and its `img-src` and `font-src` where the document's images and fonts, and the packs' files, are.
+
+A component's own drawing may need inline styles: most libraries that draw, Mermaid among them, write `style` attributes and `<style>` elements while they lay out, and measure with them. The host contract promises a component that they apply in its box (spec §7.4). The frames keep that promise by their own policy, so in host mode every strict and permissive component draws as in any page, under your page's policy too. Only your trusted components mount in your page: under a strict policy, keep a component that needs inline styles in a frame, by giving its pack a sandbox level, rather than loosening your page, since inline styles let markup injected into it draw over it and send what it reads out. A trusted component of yours puts no CSS from a param in the page, as it puts no HTML: a param's text in a `style` attribute is a document's author styling your app.
