 - **A component's text is in its box's type.** The type params (section 9.2), the box's own and those it inherits, reach the component two ways, both defaults it may ignore. What it draws with CSS inherits them from `target`; a processor that runs the component elsewhere than the page (a sandbox's frame) gives the frame's root the type the box shows, so what it draws there inherits it as it would in the page. And the context has it as values, `type`: `family`, `size` (in pixels), `weight`, `style`, `color`, `lineHeight` and `letterSpacing`, as CSS computes them, for a component that sets its text's type itself, as one that draws on a `<canvas>` does. When the box's type changes, the processor sets `type` and calls `update`.
 - **A component's natural size** is its size when its box gives it none, as CSS sizes content: a drawing with an aspect ratio takes the width it is given and its height from the ratio, and is 300 pixels wide where nothing gives it a width either (in a row laid out by content, inline); a drawing with no aspect ratio is 300 by 150 pixels, as an HTML `iframe` or `canvas` is; content that flows is as tall as its lines. A drawing sized in script from its box SHOULD contain its size (CSS `contain: size`), so that what it drew last never counts as its natural size: otherwise a box stretched once never shrinks again.
 - **Where a component runs is invisible to layout.** A processor that runs a component somewhere other than the document that lays it out (a sandbox's frame, section 15) MUST lay its use out as if it ran there: the box the same size, `target` the box's inside, the box's params applied once, and what the component draws the same size in the same place. Content larger than a box with a height is clipped at the box's edge, unless the box has `scroll` (section 9.2).
+- **Inline styles apply in a component's box.** In the document `target` is in, a `style` attribute and a `<style>` element that the component, or a library it uses, writes apply as in any page, and a processor that renders HTML MUST provide that wherever it mounts a component: so a component styles and measures what it draws as in any page, and is written no differently for any host. A sandbox's frame provides it by its own policy (section 15). A host whose page refuses inline styles (a content security policy without `'unsafe-inline'` in `style-src`) keeps the components it would mount there in frames, by the trust level it gives their packs, or allows inline styles where they run; it SHOULD NOT loosen its page's policy only for them, since inline styles let markup injected into a page draw over it and send what it reads out.
 - **Slots are rendered by the processor**, into elements the component places in its own DOM. Components inside a slot are mounted by the processor and stay live, whatever framework the parent uses.
 - **A slot is a list of items**, one per top-level block (section 9.1): its element, and, when the block is a component, its name and params. A tabs component reads its tabs' labels from the items' params. A use of a component still on its way (section 4.5) is an item whose `component` has its name, no params, and `pending: true`. An item's element is a box the processor makes around the block, so a block placed by position (section 9.2) is placed in its item, whatever the component's own markup; the component sizes it.
 - **An item's width is the component's to give.** An item is the nearest box of the block in it, so when that block uses breakpoints, the item is the box they follow and doesn't take its width from its content (section 9.3). A component that lays its items out by their content, such as a toolbar that puts them side by side, gives such an item a width. A processor can't check this, since only the component knows its layout.
@@ -1183,13 +1184,14 @@ Built-ins are an ordinary pack, `@compmd/core`, imported into every document. It
 
 | Component  | Defined as    | Lays out children                                                   | Own params                                                                                                                                  |
